1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Source: Wikiepida: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel_Axel:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriel Axel (18 April 1918 – 9 February 2014)[1] was a Danish film director, actor, writer and producer, best known for Babette's Feast (1987), which he wrote and directed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Århus, Denmark, on April 18, 1918, Axel spent most of his childhood in France, and returned to Denmark to train as an actor at the Royal Danish Theatre. After spending some years in Paris, working with the Louis Jouvet the
2 CONC atre ensemble, Axel concentrated his efforts on directing for the theatre, television and cinema in Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT With some 16 feature films to his credits Axel returned to France where he directed several large projects for television, which brought him a number of distinguished honours.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1988, Gabriel Axel won an Academy Award for his Danish feature film Babette's Feast, an adaptation of the Isak Dinesen novel. Considered his masterpiece, it took Axel 14 years to complete the project.
2 CONT
2 CONT His other films include the popular comedy Familien Gyldenkål (1975) and its sequel; a series of sexually oriented features including the campaigning Det kære legetøj (1968) which advocated the legalisation of pornography in Denma
2 CONC rk; as well as a series of Shakespearean Viking dramas, including Den røde kappe (1967), which received the Grand Prix de la Technique at the 1967 Cannes Film Festival.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2003 Gabriel Axel received a Lifetime Achievement Award at the Copenhagen International Film Festival. In 2012 he received the Rungstedlund Award.
2 CONT
2 CONT Axel died in his sleep on 9 February 2014 at the age of 95.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilde: Gyldendals Store Danske Leksikon: Gabriel Axel, 18.4.1918-9.2.2014, dansk skuespiller, film- og tv-instruktør. Gabriel Axel blev født i Frankrig og havde en stor del af sin karriere der. Han var en af de hyppigst anvendte i
2 CONC nstruktører i det tidlige danske tv-teater. Hans ca. 50 tv-produktioner 1951-70 omfatter både klassisk og moderne dramatik, herunder den første danske tv-føljeton Regnvejr og ingen penge (1965). Men hans kunstnerisk mest betydelig
2 CONC e tv-produktion er fransk, fx La Ronde de nuit (1977, Nattevagten).
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriel Axels indsats som filminstruktør er mangfoldig og af vekslende kvalitet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han modtog som den første danske instruktør en  Oscar for filmatiseringen af  Karen Blixens Babettes gæstebud (1987), hvor  Stéphane Audran spiller hovedrollen som den store franske madkunstner. Filmen er optaget i  Kulturkanon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriel Axels øvrige danske filmproduktion omfatter så forskellige genrer som folkekomedien Altid ballade (1955), realismen med En kvinde er overflødig (1957) efter  Knud Sønderbys roman, det erotiske lystspil Det tossede paradis 
2 CONC (1962) samt de historiske dramaer Den røde kappe (1967) og Prince of Jutland (1994, Prinsen af Jylland), begge efter  Saxo.
2 CONT
2 CONT I filmen Leïla (2001), som blev optaget i Marokko, fortæller Axel uden brug af dialog en tragisk kærlighedshistorie om kultursammenstød.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Wikipeida: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel_Axel: In English: Gabriel Axel: Born: 18 April 1918 in Aarhus, Denmark 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 9 February 2014 (aged 95) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: Film director 
2 CONT
2 CONT Years active: 1951 - 2001 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriel Axel (18 April 1918 – 9 February 2014) was a Danish film director, actor, writer and producer, best known for Babette's Feast (1987), which he wrote and directed.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Århus, Denmark, on April 18, 1918, Axel spent most of his childhood in France, and returned to Denmark to train as an actor at the Royal Danish Theatre. After spending some years in Paris, working with the Louis Jouvet the
2 CONC atre ensemble, Axel concentrated his efforts on directing for the theatre, television and cinema in Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT With some 16 feature films to his credits Axel returned to France where he directed several large projects for television, which brought him a number of distinguished honours.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1988, Gabriel Axel won an Academy Award for his Danish feature film Babette's Feast, an adaptation of the Isak Dinesen novel.
2 CONT
2 CONT His other films include the popular comedy Familien Gyldenkål (1975) and its sequel; a series of sexually oriented features including the campaigning Det kære legetøj (1968) which advocated the legalisation of pornography in Denma
2 CONC rk; as well as a series of Shakespearean Viking dramas, including Den røde kappe (1967), which received the Grand Prix de la Technique at the 1967 Cannes Film Festival.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2003, Gabriel Axel received a Lifetime Achievement Award at the Copenhagen International Film Festival.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2012 he received the Rungstedlund Award.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in his sleep on 9 February 2014.[3].
