1 OCCU Poet & Novelist, Danish author (Dansk forfatter), 1917 Nobelprismodtager i litteratur
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Danish Wikipeida in Danish: https://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karl_Gjellerup
2 CONT
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karl_Adolph_Gjellerup '''Karl Gjellerup'''] (June 2, 1857 – October 13, 1919) was a Danish poet and novelist who together with his compatriot [http://www.geni.com/people/Henrik-Pontoppidan/60000000145
2 CONC 62415422 Henrik Pontoppidan] won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1917. He belonged to the Modern Break-Through. He occasionally used the pseudonym Epigonos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gjellerup was the son of a vicar in Zealand and grew up in a national and romantic idealistic atmosphere. In the 1870s he broke with his background and at first he became an enthusiastic supporter of the naturalist movement and Ge
2 CONC org Brandes, writing audacious novels about free love and atheism. Strongly influenced by his origin he gradually left the Brandes line and 1885 he broke totally with the naturalists, becoming a new romanticist. A central trace of
2 CONC  his life was his Germanophile attitude, he felt himself strongly attracted to German culture (his wife was a German) and 1892 he finally settled in Germany, which made him unpopular in Denmark on both the right and left wing. As 
2 CONC years passed he totally identified with the German Empire, including its war aims 1914-18.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the early works of Gjellerup must be mentioned his most important novel Germanernes Lærling (1882, i.e. The Learner of German), a partly autobiographic tale of the development of a young man from being a conformist theologia
2 CONC n to a pro-German atheist and intellectual, and Minna (1889), on the surface, a love story but more of a study in woman's psychology. Some Wagnerian dramas show his growing romanticist interests. An important work is the novel Møl
2 CONC len (1896, i. e. The Mill), a sinister melodrama of love and jealousy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Later years'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In his last years he was clearly influenced by Buddhism and Oriental culture. His critically acclaimed work Der Pilger Kamanita/Pilgrimen Kamanita (1906, i.e. The Pilgrim Kamanita) has been called 'one of the oddest novels written
2 CONC  in Danish'. It features the journey of Kamanita, an Indian merchant's son, from earthly prosperity and carnal romance, through the ups and downs of the world's way, a chance meeting with a stranger monk (who, unbeknownst to Kaman
2 CONC ita, was actually Gautama Buddha), death, and reincarnation towards nirvana. In Thailand, which is a Buddhist country, The Pilgrim Kamanita is on the school programme.
2 CONT
2 CONT Den fuldendtes hustru (1907, i.e. The wife of the perfect) is a versified drama, inspired by Dante's Divine Comedy, about Buddha's earthly life as Siddharta, being inhibited in his spiritual efforts by his wife, Yasodhara. The gia
2 CONC nt novel Verdensvandrerne (1910, i.e. The world roamers) takes its contemporary starting point in a German female academic on a study tour in India, but evolves across chronological levels, in which characters re-experience what h
2 CONC as happened in former eons, thus featuring souls roaming from one incarnation to another.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rudolph Stens Landpraksis (1913, i.e. The country practice of [physician] Rudolph Sten) is set in the rural Zealand of Gjellerup's youth. The main character develops from a liberal, superficial outlook on life, including youthful 
2 CONC romantical conflicts, through years of reflection and asketic devotion to duty towards a more mature standpoint, hinting at the author's own course of life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Das heiligste Tier (1919, i.e. The holiest animal) was Gjellerup's last work. Having elements of self-parody, it is regarded as his only attempt at humour. It is a peculiar mythological satire in which animals arrive at their own 
2 CONC Elysium after death. These include the snake that killed Cleopatra, Odysseus' dog Argos, Wisvamitra (the holy cow of India), the donkey of Jesus and the horses of various historical commanders in field. The assembly select, after 
2 CONC discussion, Buddha's horse Kantaka as the holiest of animals, but it has left without a trace to follow its master to nirvana.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Aftermath'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In Denmark, Gjellerup's Nobel award was received with little enthusiasm. He had long been regarded as a German writer. During various stages of his career, he had made himself unpopular with both the naturalist left surrounding Ge
2 CONC org Brandes and the conservative right. His nomination for the Nobel award was, however, supported from Danish side for several times. Because Sweden was neutral during World War I, the divided prize did not arouse political specu
2 CONC lations about partial decision, but showed on the other hand allegiance between the Nordic neighbors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Today Gjellerup is almost forgotten in Denmark. In spite of this, however, literary historians normally regard him as an honest seeker after truth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gjellerup's works have been translated into several languages, including German (often translated by himself), Swedish, English, Dutch, Polish, Thai and others[citation needed]. The Pilgrim Kamanita is his most widely translated b
2 CONC ook, having been published in several European countries and the United States[citation needed]. In Thailand, a Buddhist country, the first half of the Thai translation of Kamanita have been used in secondary school textbooks.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''See also:''' [http://www.nobelprize.org/nobel_prizes/literature/laureates/1917/gjellerup-autobio.html# The Nobel Prize in Literature 1917, '''Karl Gjellerup, Henrik Pontoppidan] '''
