1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Rasmus attended university before marrying. He owned Brendstrup Overgaard in Tilst Parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTES ON BRENDSTRUP
2 CONT
2 CONT There are several references throughout this chapter to "Brendstrup" and/or variations of this name. What follows are some details concerning the ownership/lesseeship/usership of these so-named lands/farms that have been taken fro
2 CONC m various Danish records.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is important to note, however , that there are two different farms often referred to as "Brendstrup" and while the distinction is not always clear, the following definitions will help separate these two distinctly different par
2 CONC cels of land.
2 CONT
2 CONT A - BRENDSTRUP OVERGAARD (sometimes referred to as "Over Brendstrupgaard" or as "Brendstrupgaard")
2 CONT
2 CONT Strictly speaking, this means "topmost" or "over" but most likely means "main" farm. It seems to have been administered separately from "Brendstrup Nedergaard" for most of it's history. "Brendstrup" was a town located in Hasle Dis
2 CONC trict, Tilst Parish, Aarhus County and the farms took their name from this town which was near the farms discussed here.
2 CONT
2 CONT B - BRENDSTRUP NEDERGAARD (sometimes referred to as "Neder Brendstrup"
2 CONT
2 CONT A strict translation of this would yield "Brendstrup under-farm" but it would seem logical to interpret this as "Lesser Brendstrup" --- most likely a designated portion of the main farm that was subleased and farmed separately fro
2 CONC m the main farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following descriptions, reference is made to "lessee" and "user". Frequently, such farm lands were owned by one person who in turn, either leased(to a "lessee") the land or a portion thereof to someone else. This "lessee" m
2 CONC ight work the land himself but sometimes hired someone else to work the land. This third party was known as a "user" in such cases. The owner might bypass the "lessee" stage of such arrangements and engage a "user" directly himsel
2 CONC f. The distinction between a "lessee" and a "user" was that the "lessee" leased the land and paid the owner a pre-arranged sum (often in the farm's produce and/or cash) on a yearly basis. Any profit or surplus from the lands would
2 CONC  be retained by the "lessee". A "user" would pay the "lessee" or "owner" a pre-arranged sum but would not retain for himself any of the surplus or profit. He worked the land for an agreed fee (either cash or produce) for his labor
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the original Danish documents from which this data has been taken, the size of various land parcels was made in "tønders". This was an old Danish unit of land measurement. It literally means "barrel" but in more practical terms
2 CONC , it meant the area of land that could be seeded with one barrel of corn. The conversion of this unit of measurement into land-area units in current terms is: 1 tønder = 1 barrel = 1.363 acres. For clarity's sake, I've expressed a
2 CONC ll land area units in acres only.
2 CONT
2 CONT BRENDSTRUP OVERGAARD - HISTORY (main farm)
2 CONT
2 CONT For a period up to 1664, Brendstrup Overgaard was owned by UFFE SKADE. (He also owned Brendstrup Nedergaard at this time). The main farm was 29.986 acres plus other undescribed belongings. UFFE SKADE leased approximately 21.127 ac
2 CONC res of this land to LAURITS (lars) JENSEN for the following sum.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		5 barrels 			- 	Barley 		5 barrels 			- 	Oats 		1(unknown measure) 	- 	Unknown 		1 				- 	Hog 		1 				- 	Sheep 		1 				- 	Lamb 		1 				- 	Goose 		1 				- 	Chicken 		cash 				- 	1 Mark 		1 (unknown measure) 	- 	Malt (beer?)
2 CONT
2 CONT Laurits (Lars) JENSEN was twice married and had several children....some of whom became rather outstanding citizens in Aarhus as grocers and public trustees. One of these children is known by name to us from the court papers conce
2 CONC rning the winding up of Laurits' estate. This was MICHEL LASSEN who on April 16, 1711 settled his father's estate. The heirs were: all surviving sons and daughters of Laurits and the children/grandchildren of the non-surviving son
2 CONC s and daughters. MICHEL LASSEN died without children of his own. (There is no indication that he was even ever married.) [Source of this date: Probate Court Register for the city of Aarhus, Book #8 page 427 A.]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1664, ownership of Brendstrup Overgaard was purchased by CHRISTEN FRIIS. The registered user at this time was RASMUS PEDERSEN...most likely by arrangement with LAURITS (Lars) JENSEN.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1687 the user is shown to have been RASMUS LASSEN. He was born in Brendstrup in 1639 and died on March 10, 1687. He was married to JOHANNE NIELSDATTER who was born around 1643 and died in Brendstrup on October 23, 1692. Rasmus 
2 CONC and Johanne were married for 22 years and had six sons and four daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1688, after the death of her husband, Johanne was the listed user. She acquired an additional 16.320 acres of land which were added to Brendstrup Overgaard. As of 1692, the farm was split into two parcels of land and each had d
2 CONC ifferent users.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first parcel of land was "used" by ANDERS RASMUSSEN LASSEN. He was born in Brendstrup in 1660 and was buried on July 7, 1724 in Tilst Parish. On February 12, 1690, he married MARGRETHE OLUFSDATTER in Aarhus Cathedral. She was 
2 CONC born in 1668 in Aarhus, the daughter of grocer OLUF PEDERSEN from Skjødstrup. She died in Brendstrup in 1751 and was buried on May 13 in Tilst Parish. From the death of her husband in 1724 until 1736, she was the registered "user"
2 CONC  of this first portion of Brendstrup Overgaard.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1736 her son RASMUS ANDERSEN LASSEN (father of METTE JOHANNE RASMUSDATTER who was the first matriarch of the Faurschou family) became the registered "lessee" [Source of this date: Lessee protocol for FRIISENBORG, 1736]
2 CONT
2 CONT RASMUS ANDERSEN LASSEN was born in 1715, christened in Tilst Church on November 3, 17?? and died in 1766. He was buried on January 6th of that year. His wife was APOLLONE SØRENSDATTER who was born on Søndergaard Farm in Laasby Par
2 CONC ish. Apollone died in Brendstrup Overgaard in 1786 and was buried on September 10. Apollone's parents were SØRE PEDERSEN and ANNA MORTENDATTER who farmed Søndergaard Farm in Laasby Parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT As for the second portion of the farm, from 1692 to 1726, the registered user was POUL RASMUSSEN who was married to METTE RASMUSDATTER (she was the sister of ANDERS RASMUSSEN LASSEN).
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1726 to 1732, the registered user was RASMUS POULSEN (most likely the son of Poul Rasmussen).
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1732 to 1766, CHRISTEN PEDERSEN BORUM, son-in-law to ANDERS RASMUSSEN LASSEN was registered as the "lessee" of this portion of Brendstrup Overgaard.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1766, RASMUS ANDERSEN LASSEN owned both halves of Brendstrup Overgaard. He must have bought this land from CHRISTEN FRIIS sometime between 1736 and 1766. It would seem that sometime around this period, the custom of hav
2 CONC ing "lessees" and "users" was discontinued since all subsequent data shows "ownership" only.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death in 1766, ownership passed to ANDERS RASMUSSEN LASSEN. This is NOT the same ANDERS RASMUSSEN LASSEN who was the registered "user" of the first portion of Brendstrup Overgaard from 1692 to 1724 as mentioned above. Thi
2 CONC s second ANDERS RASMUSSEN LASSEN was born in 1739 and lived until 1808. While it is not absolutely sure who this second ANDERS RASMUSSEN LASSEN's parents were, it is fairly safe to assume they were the RASMUS ANDERSEN LASSEN and A
2 CONC POLLONE SØRENSDATTER. That would make the first ANDERS RASMUSSEN LASSEN the grandfather of this second ANDERS RASMUSSEN LASSEN.
2 CONT
2 CONT This second ANDERS RASMUSSEN LASSEN was twice married: first to MAREN SØRENSDATTER who lived from 1748 to 1803; second marriage to JOHANNE CATHRINE BAGGE who lived from 1787 to 1862.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1809, after the death of the second ANDERS RASMUSSEN LASSEN, his second wife (JOHANNE CATHRINE BAGGE) married RASMUS FAURSCHOU (#5) and he became the registered owner of Brendstrup Overgaard. RASMUS FAURSCHOU managed the farm u
2 CONC ntil his retirement in 1857. During his years of ownership, the farm was expanded: he added 17.719 acres in 1844 and a further 27.260 acres in 1850.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1857 JENS LASSEN FAURSCHOU became the owner of these lands. The son of RASMUS and JOHANNE CATHRINE FAURSCHOU, he continued to expand the farm's holdings. One of his acquisitions was the 17.719 acre farm of Mr. C. RAMSING of Kol
2 CONC dkærgaard in the Brendstrup area. It seems that JENS LASSEN FAURSCHOU distributed some of his acquisitions between Brendstrup Overgaard and Brendstrup Nedergaard, both of which he owned at this time.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1893, JENS LASSEN FAURSCHOU  passed Brendstrup Overgaard onto his son-in-law POUL POULSEN of Rolsøgaard who was married to Jen's daughter, ANNA JOHANNE OSTERFELDT FAURSCHOU POUL POULSEN was born in ASMUSGAARD on July 28, 1870. 
2 CONC At the time of Poul's taking possession of Brendstrup Overgaard the farm's holdings were listed as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT 			main farm 			6.120 acres 			Windmill and house 	4.771 acres 			house 			1.363 acres 			two house 			0.000 acres 			forest in Trige Parish 	91.321 acres 			TOTAL 			133.575 acres
2 CONT
2 CONT Half of this forest area was in the Tilst Parish Royal Grants. At this time in Danish history, Royal Grants were lands granted by the crown to certain churches. Such lands could be used by the church for future expansion or could 
2 CONC be sold for profit.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1913, the farm was bought by CHRISTIAN BRISEL
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1916, J.L. ROTBOLL bought the farm
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1924, Aage PEDERSEN became it's owner.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to a 1961 registry, Brendstrup Overgaard consisted of 511.909 acres
2 CONT
2 CONT BRENDSTRUP NEDERGAARD HISTORY
2 CONT
2 CONT As of 1664, the owner of Brendstrup Nedergaard was UFFE SKADE and its user was LAURITS (LARS) JENSEN. These are the same people who owned/used Brendstrup Overgaard at this time. Brendstrup Nedergaard was 20.445 acres at this time.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1668, ownership was in the hands of SHERIFF BIELKE and the authorized user was SØFREN SØFRENSEN.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1740, JENS ANDERSEN LASSEN became it's owner and purchased additional lands in Friisenborg to add to Brendstrup Nedergaard. JENS ANDERSEN LASSEN lived from 1705 to 1776.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1772, his son, ANDERS JENSEN LASSEN took over the farm. Born in 1742, his first marriage was to his cousin. CATHRINE RASMUSDATTER LASSEN. She was the sister of ANDERS RASMUSSEN LASSEN. who was the registered user of Brendstrup 
2 CONC Overgaard. ANDERS JENSEN LASSEN's second marriage was to ANNA PEDERSDATTER. He died in 1801.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1797, ANDERS JENSEN HVIID sold the farm to JØRGEN MØRCH SECHER. He was born September 22, 1774 and died August 8, 1817. His father was ANCHER SECHER and his mother was MARIE MØRCH. At the same time as he bought Brendstrup Neder
2 CONC gaard, he purchased the Skejby Parish and Brendstrup Town Royal Grants.
2 CONT
2 CONT JØRGEN MØRCH SECHER sold Brendstrup Nedergaard in 1816 to JENS LASSEN FAURSCHOU (#4). JENS LASSEN FAURSCHOU was married to MAREN THUESEN and as of the 1834 census, they were living on the farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of JENS LASSEN FAURSCHOU in 1834, the farm's ownership passed to his brother OLE FAURSCHOU since Jens and Maren were childless. OLE FAURSCHOU was married to FREDERIKKE FERDINANDINE DAHL. Their daughter NICOLINE late
2 CONC r married JØRGEN MØRCH SECHER but this was not the same JØRGEN MØRCH SECHER as mentioned in previous paragraphs above. Upon examination of the dates involved here, it seems safe to assume that this second JØRGEN MØRCH SECHER was t
2 CONC he son of that first mentioned JØRGEN MØRCH SECHER.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1862, OLE FAURSCHOU sold Brendstrup Nedergaard to his brother's (RASMUS FAURSCHOU son. This was JENS LASSEN FAURSCHOU (#5I) and he was also the owner of Brendstrup Overgaard at the same time.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1895, JENS LASSEN FAURSCHOU sold Brendstrup Nedergaard to his son-in-law, POUL POULSEN who had taken over Brendstrup Overgaard two years before.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following data is the results of various official censuses which related to Brendstrup Overgaard. Tilst Parish, Aarhus county
2 CONT
2 CONT 1787 CENSUS 											 Anders Rasmussen LASSEN (the second one) 	48 years old 		farm owner Maren Sørensdatter LASSEN 				37 years old 		his wife Anna Elizabeth 						11 years old 		daughter Apollone 							8 years old 		daughter 
2 CONC Rasmus 							5 years old		Son 					
2 CONT
2 CONT 							 											 1801 CENSUS 											 Anders Rasmussen LASSEN (the second one) 	61 years old		farm owner Maren Sørensdatter LASSEN 				51 years old 		his wife Anna Elizabeth 			         			24 years old 		daughter Apollone 		
2 CONC 		         			21 years old 		daughter Anders JENSEN 			         			12 years old 		nephew 											 											 1834 CENSUS 											 Rasmus FAURSCHOU 				55 years old			farm owner Johanne Cathrine FAURSCHOU 			45 years old 			
2 CONC his wife Mette Johanne (Hanne)      			23 years old 			daughter Nicoline Cathrine       				22 years old 			daughter Maren       						19 years old 			daughter Andreas             					15 years old 			son Anna         						14 yea
2 CONC rs old 			daughter Niels          						12 years old 			son Jens Lassen 						11 years old 			son
2 CONT
2 CONT This 1834 census also listed one housekeeper and 8 farms hands as residents of Brendstrup Overgaard. This 1834 census for Brendstrup Nedergaard listed the following residents
2 CONT
